## Proof

Let $\theta = 2\pi/n$ and index $j,k \in \{1,2,\dots,n\}$. Using the angle-addition identity,
$$a_{jk} = \cos(j\theta + k\theta) = \cos(j\theta)\cos(k\theta) - \sin(j\theta)\sin(k\theta).$$
Define vectors $\mathbf{u}, \mathbf{v} \in \mathbb{R}^n$ by $u_j = \cos(j\theta)$, $v_j = \sin(j\theta)$. Then
$$A = \mathbf{u}\mathbf{u}^T - \mathbf{v}\mathbf{v}^T.$$
Set $X = [\mathbf{u}\;\mathbf{v}]$ and $Y = [\mathbf{u}\;-\mathbf{v}]$ (both $n\times 2$), so $A = XY^T$. By Sylvester's determinant identity,
$$\det(I + A) = \det(I_2 + Y^T X).$$

**Computing the inner products.** Since $e^{i\cdot 4\pi j/n}$, $j=1,\dots,n$, are $n$-th roots of unity (with $e^{i4\pi/n}\neq 1$ for $n\ge 3$), their sum is $0$, giving $\sum_{j=1}^n \cos(4\pi j/n) = 0$ and $\sum_{j=1}^n \sin(4\pi j/n) = 0$. Therefore
$$\mathbf{u}^T\mathbf{u} = \sum_{j=1}^n \cos^2(j\theta) = \frac{n}{2} + \frac{1}{2}\sum_{j=1}^n\cos(2j\theta) = \frac{n}{2},$$
$$\mathbf{v}^T\mathbf{v} = \sum_{j=1}^n \sin^2(j\theta) = \frac{n}{2} - \frac{1}{2}\sum_{j=1}^n\cos(2j\theta) = \frac{n}{2},$$
$$\mathbf{u}^T\mathbf{v} = \frac{1}{2}\sum_{j=1}^n \sin(2j\theta) = 0.$$

Hence
$$Y^T X = \begin{pmatrix} \mathbf{u}^T\mathbf{u} & \mathbf{u}^T\mathbf{v} \\ -\mathbf{v}^T\mathbf{u} & -\mathbf{v}^T\mathbf{v} \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} n/2 & 0 \\ 0 & -n/2 \end{pmatrix},$$
and
$$I_2 + Y^T X = \begin{pmatrix} 1+n/2 & 0 \\ 0 & 1-n/2 \end{pmatrix}.$$

Taking the determinant,
$$\det(I+A) = \left(1+\frac{n}{2}\right)\left(1-\frac{n}{2}\right) = 1 - \frac{n^2}{4}.$$

**Verification ($n=3$).** With $\theta=2\pi/3$: $\mathbf{u}=(-\tfrac12,-\tfrac12,1)^T$, $\mathbf{v}=(\tfrac{\sqrt3}{2},-\tfrac{\sqrt3}{2},0)^T$; inner products give $\tfrac32,\tfrac32,0$ as predicted, and $\det(I+A)=(1+\tfrac32)(1-\tfrac32)=-\tfrac54 = 1-\tfrac{9}{4}$. ✓

$$\boxed{\det(I+A) = 1 - \dfrac{n^2}{4}}$$

